\section{Columns}{
The columns of the data are the object's filters. \code{names(x)} lists them,
\code{dim(x)}, \code{nrow(x)}, and \code{ncol(x)} describe the data, and
\code{as.data.frame(x)} returns it.
\itemize{
\item \code{x$col} creates the input for one column, named exactly.
\item \code{x[[cols]]} creates the inputs for the selected columns. One column gives
the input itself, as \code{$} does; several give a \code{\link[shiny:tagList]{shiny::tagList()}} in the
order selected.
\item \code{x[cols]} returns a \code{shinyfilters} object with only the selected columns,
in the order selected. They keep their inputs, arguments, and markers,
and the object keeps its defaults and namespace.
}

\code{$} and \code{[[} create the inputs \code{\link[=filterInput]{filterInput()}} creates for those columns,
with the object's defaults, namespace, and \code{\link[=with_filters]{with_filters()}} choices applied.

\code{cols} uses <\code{\link[tidyselect:language]{tidy-select}}> in both \code{[} and \code{[[}:
bare names, strings, positions, \code{c()}, \code{!}, and helpers such as
\code{starts_with()}. Use \code{all_of()} to select with a character vector stored in
a variable. A selection must match at least one column, and neither
operator takes rows.

\code{\link[dplyr:select]{dplyr::select()}} works like \code{[}, and \code{\link[dplyr:pull]{dplyr::pull()}} like \code{$}; see
\link{shinyfilters-dplyr}.

Assigning with \verb{$<-}, \verb{[[<-}, or \verb{@<-} is an error.
