\section{In an app}{
Place the object in a UI like any tag. It creates its inputs when the page
is rendered, as \code{filterInput(x)} does: one input per column, in column
order. An input's id is its column's name, prefixed with the namespace.
Place \code{x[cols]} to put some of the filters in one part of the page.

\code{$}, \code{[[}, and \code{\link[=filterInput]{filterInput()}} create the inputs right away instead. Two
cases need them:
\itemize{
\item A UI function that inspects its contents before the page is rendered,
such as \code{bslib::accordion()}, errors when given the object. Give it
\code{x[[cols]]}.
\item An app with bookmarking enabled errors when the object is placed in its
UI, because inputs created when the page is rendered don't restore their
bookmarked values. Create the inputs inside the UI function. Inside a
session, such as in \code{\link[shiny:renderUI]{shiny::renderUI()}}, the object works as it is.
}

In the server, pass the object to \code{\link[=shinyfilters_server]{shinyfilters_server()}}. It keeps each
input's choices in line with the other filters, and returns the filtered
